# Spring Boot Auto-Configuration

## What it is
Auto-configuration is one of Spring Boot's most powerful features. It automatically configures your Spring application based on the dependencies on your classpath. For example, adding `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` causes Spring Boot to automatically configure a `DataSource`, persistence manager, and related components, so you don't write that boilerplate yourself.

## How it works
- Spring Boot scans the classpath and applies relevant auto-configurations listed in each starter's `META-INF/spring.factories` file.
- Auto-configurations are ordinary `@Configuration` classes guarded by conditional annotations.
- `@EnableAutoConfiguration` switches this mechanism on. You rarely declare it directly because it is bundled into `@SpringBootApplication`, which combines `@Configuration`, `@EnableAutoConfiguration`, and `@ComponentScan`.

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

## Conditional annotations: example
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnClass({DataSource.class, JdbcTemplate.class})
@EnableConfigurationProperties(JdbcProperties.class)
@AutoConfigureAfter(DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
public class JdbcTemplateAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource dataSource) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(dataSource);
    }
}
```
- `@ConditionalOnClass`: applies the configuration only if the listed classes are on the classpath.
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: registers the bean only if you haven't defined one yourself, so your beans always take precedence over the defaults.
- `@EnableConfigurationProperties`: binds externalized properties to a properties class.
- `@AutoConfigureAfter`: controls ordering relative to other auto-configurations.

## Config demo (simulated)
Java can't run in the Python sandbox, so I ran a Python simulation of the conditional logic above. It showed three scenarios:
1. **No user bean, classes present:** `@ConditionalOnClass` matched and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` matched, so the auto-configured `JdbcTemplate` was registered.
2. **User defines their own `JdbcTemplate`:** auto-configuration backed off, and the user's bean was used.
3. **`JdbcTemplate` not on the classpath:** the configuration was skipped entirely.

This mirrors how Spring Boot decides which beans to create. Note that it is a simplified model of the decision logic, not actual Spring execution.

